#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/id #uuid "283001f5-f9e4-5990-a900-318677a48601" :question/math-academy-id "e-3740" :question/problem "Determine the intervals on which the function $f(x) = x^3 -3x$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "006fe48a-9c8e-568b-90f1-40b12dc338bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3741" :question/problem "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "99e07b9d-aaaa-54b6-9118-78df023261f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3745" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "635742ee-51f9-52cc-9da0-9449b0337897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is concave up nowhere." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293675} {:answer/id #uuid "5decf4a0-9685-5b59-9536-adb0c5339bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293676} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0d60d4-c808-524a-bd39-cfa7eb24e8a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293677} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b7b74a-d832-51e5-8564-65f071fc3ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293678} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb500c6-43db-5af6-ae0a-64079df0da88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb500c6-43db-5af6-ae0a-64079df0da88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293679} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc25a94-cdab-5a5a-9c48-14eeb1210853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c643932b-c8cc-535d-94b8-66da48e8c389" :question/math-academy-id "q-114260" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n6x-6>0\\,⟹\\,x>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338ad745-d92e-5d74-ae7b-3720d22adfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is concave down nowhere." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293682} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbd523-d309-565b-a178-9af0d2d74f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293683} {:answer/id #uuid "21cf0eeb-d81a-5899-bdd3-d7ed197c9483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293684} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f8aa3-97c2-546c-ba84-b9400e634203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293685} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbc211b-d700-5c2b-a41c-d50d81c70814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "338ad745-d92e-5d74-ae7b-3720d22adfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function is concave down nowhere." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293682} :answer-field/id #uuid "56d1b0ff-6a07-567b-9fd3-3ca303fca709" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e55fdbac-6bb5-5bac-b529-e0d157ba40d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114264" :question/problem "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)={e}^{1-x}+2x$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)={e}^{1-x}+2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{1-x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-(-{e}^{1-x})={e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ Notice that ${f}^{″}(x)={e}^{1-x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffde426-a97f-5dd2-afa0-be7cd1c04dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293689} {:answer/id #uuid "57a153c2-4712-5abd-aeb8-86da041cec06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293690} {:answer/id #uuid "03d4f9be-e445-52f2-9e85-2b5fc15af3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293691} {:answer/id #uuid "66e76bc3-16ac-5dbd-8410-79bf932a02c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293692} {:answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293693} :answer-field/id #uuid "71107757-ea07-51d8-90b3-bf50cdd589cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7af01e6f-dc86-5725-9b0b-63602f01e9af" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a88625d-af27-53c4-a457-58e65edd2cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293696} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5399f1-9fff-5cb2-8ad0-4cb50b507b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293697} {:answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293698} {:answer/id #uuid "5a252763-0942-541b-b522-67772f90d79f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293699} {:answer/id #uuid "e74db172-d46a-5ed3-82af-e90331457394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc355f65-3bf9-5528-8a74-4acdf8839e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7ee43f3-f778-5c3e-9099-6bca7ddcc6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114271" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1c2967-fe66-5164-8106-1c48cf85e021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293703} {:answer/id #uuid "98153d91-ae04-593a-acd3-4afb60a74acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293704} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd670c-e59b-51e5-8b48-a6c89b0f0fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293705} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ab2773-bf4c-52c2-ba6c-89081c9bdd5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293706} {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f1e316f-78a1-538e-a241-00deb8b4f4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0fa5633-3d2b-5f5b-a938-ef10da4c0590" :question/math-academy-id "q-114277" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863515ff-8ffa-5b28-9838-02b3f2ee569d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183005} {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} {:answer/id #uuid "e526471f-734d-50c7-8111-39708dfcf1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- ∞,-4)$ and $(0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183007} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b7fc8b-8aa3-5225-8b1a-b1142bd31a58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183008} {:answer/id #uuid "3f44e70e-9e92-5956-86ee-8ffee243e87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4, 0)$ and $(4, ∞)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8974190b-3ce8-54d8-b280-96db74be70b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183006} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c59ab91-fe4f-538c-9b7e-7e96d0c2a5ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f627add3-7eef-5ed1-b10b-a3822778f5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31214" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/9fe57341b631d34a9dfa41619a29f816b00b41b3b07f450fd333808230835e2c.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,4)$ only. \n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,4)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2006862-cceb-5aa4-9929-c791e1b36076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293710} {:answer/id #uuid "f194090a-34e7-5c2b-abbc-19411b397161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293711} {:answer/id #uuid "020deb07-301a-5991-92aa-3b798d0d7394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293712} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa6e347-8860-58c3-a4db-e20bd94b1518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293713} {:answer/id #uuid "c44ded78-e720-5260-b84c-0946aea05651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(-2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aa6e347-8860-58c3-a4db-e20bd94b1518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293713} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2645ea0-8179-5b0f-81bb-d2dae6f901ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cdc7ba4-facb-5f35-ba6c-5c4d918c35b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-31228" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/305fa76994ff0be3c43894226eaa82d3d9e016c6add11cd48d80486c50643ed0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-4,-2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2)$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8489329c-181a-58d4-b005-7a305f54a8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,-6)$ and $(2, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183011} {:answer/id #uuid "beb56c00-8dc9-51ff-aa37-bdab349702a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2, 2)$ and $(2, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183012} {:answer/id #uuid "c708c28b-de57-51ff-a709-753771f96325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183013} {:answer/id #uuid "4a01e1e6-2f7b-544b-bbe8-361d52a4b80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-6,-2)$ and $(-2, 2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183014} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3c4026-3398-59c3-908a-96bce9667579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, 5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8489329c-181a-58d4-b005-7a305f54a8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-7,-6)$ and $(2, 5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183011} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1bd0166-c2f6-5313-b511-60e817cf684c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "481f2241-248d-5665-9ba5-3305f5c326b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-31229" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/3891159e9d53769e4f95176a2585e339ca347e39aa91c9df8cd6456f8d3155da.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "608df557-e420-5090-bc41-1b36aa8b47a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "608df557-e420-5090-bc41-1b36aa8b47a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293717} :answer-field/id #uuid "66041a7a-060b-5392-8ff9-f42202ea03d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e59b9ca-130b-5234-bfd9-b73e48f251ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e59b9ca-130b-5234-bfd9-b73e48f251ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293719} :answer-field/id #uuid "972def23-d3a0-5f35-bddb-299a30ef9d34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7f18171-79a5-5247-b3e6-61ea19eed26d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344330" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=-3{e}^{2x}+5$ is concave down.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-3{e}^{2x}+5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-6{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-12{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12{e}^{2x} & <0 \\\\ {e}^{2x} & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\,-\\infty ,\\infty \\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b982f57b-4388-574a-b450-26ddcc0d466a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293722} {:answer/id #uuid "073f8123-cb98-5c22-8816-3e60cde51af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "073f8123-cb98-5c22-8816-3e60cde51af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293723} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eee1233-c6dc-5118-9310-93fe8b149f48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f347a15-e82a-53cf-8e47-6e7a629d6456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f347a15-e82a-53cf-8e47-6e7a629d6456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293725} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2397e37-cab1-5a5a-ad83-b194b672069f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "674e1510-2c32-55cc-bff1-6ee60c46a419" :question/math-academy-id "q-344334" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=6x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)$ is concave down.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=6x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6+\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}} & <\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x>-1$ we have $(x+1)^{2}<(x+4)^{2},$ and thus $\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}>\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}<\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}$ is always true in the given domain, and we conclude that the largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave down is $(\\,-1,\\infty \\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d267a85-9025-5d21-b40e-214393ef55e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293728} {:answer/id #uuid "66fa2171-1d8e-5f9a-9333-ce9602127094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66fa2171-1d8e-5f9a-9333-ce9602127094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293729} :answer-field/id #uuid "e55671b9-82a2-5326-b519-bc17684c1d68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463a6292-e181-5420-9f32-056cae39a71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "463a6292-e181-5420-9f32-056cae39a71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293731} :answer-field/id #uuid "03da742a-0e06-546a-92c8-9f6f0039a74b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37de4824-560d-5bed-91bb-680586ddf82b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344340" :question/problem "Determine the **largest** interval on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}$ is concave up.\n\nThe largest interval where $f(x)$ is concave up is\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\ne -2.$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x)(2x+4)-2{x}^{2}}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x(x+4)}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x}{(2x+4)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{(4x+8)(2x+4)^{2}-4(2{x}^{2}+8x)(2x+4)}{(2x+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(x+2)^{2}-8{x}^{2}-32x}{(2x+4)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}^{2}+32x+32-8{x}^{2}-32x}{(2x+4)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{3}} \\\\  & =4(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+2)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ (x+2)^{-3} & >0 \\\\ x+2 & >0 \\\\ x & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\,-2,\\infty \\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c28336b-f7c0-5a09-964d-ad3bdebc6b17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293734} {:answer/id #uuid "590a86a7-7b83-5e23-b212-b72dd4a821bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293735} {:answer/id #uuid "ff08702c-faac-5647-937f-5eae3fc72903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293736} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf0a318-778e-552c-bf70-7cffc28f8051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293737} {:answer/id #uuid "ebeca85c-2e86-52b1-b789-2516911f742b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf0a318-778e-552c-bf70-7cffc28f8051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293737} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c615065-85e9-5941-ae1b-b5ba93c5bde0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf81ab16-a4e9-51ca-976b-3909948534cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-78960" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $[0,\\pi ].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0$ when $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi .$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a25d1931-aa16-5f48-aac8-16feb83f1b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293741} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3a5e5d-4fa1-57b8-ba5a-b8db04685089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,0),$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293742} {:answer/id #uuid "32a0146c-5e92-5110-9f01-c29f6967b287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293743} {:answer/id #uuid "3a299bb8-e651-537f-9a52-b32e10309107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293744} {:answer/id #uuid "4a68f172-8a9e-5780-acd3-ef8cce6a9ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c3a5e5d-4fa1-57b8-ba5a-b8db04685089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,0),$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293742} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b649b3-12ba-59ba-9d07-c589ecc9a218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b3e1e22-14d1-593b-b595-c9569063a41e" :question/math-academy-id "q-78970" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/42a77771cd98666593703eebd649050f12995e3edc08a0077e8e10ae68415c50.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={g}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,0),$ and $(1,3).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,0),$ and $(1,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of the Second Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2da17ba-aa6d-5c9c-897c-f778a7536e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293748} {:answer/id #uuid "1c3c4fd5-7535-5c87-83a2-16879f792cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and  $(1,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293749} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4a196e-51cb-53e7-9fb9-21c5f8165c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-2)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293750} {:answer/id #uuid "120af96c-6c1d-53db-b301-069d1d5a7ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293751} {:answer/id #uuid "e778a44a-84b3-5e61-851d-db045cb9bb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c3c4fd5-7535-5c87-83a2-16879f792cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-5,-1)$ and  $(1,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293749} :answer-field/id #uuid "875bf0f0-d8f9-5c3f-a802-e37ba5f836e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1c4b1dc-3c19-531f-b884-2df171ebf91d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79038" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/ab75b847928c038f996b0915ebc87c8a05b025c2a2c20f77429cd8e9b6c85f0d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and  $(1,5).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and  $(1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5aa965-8afa-5415-af0b-d1b2a70d19d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293755} {:answer/id #uuid "5e181f05-c15f-5f8b-9d6d-ca8102d59c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293756} {:answer/id #uuid "852e8b94-892d-511b-8eaa-81b95b81cc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293757} {:answer/id #uuid "63d3657e-ae5f-516e-9276-b94fb8b180c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293758} {:answer/id #uuid "44adaf0e-0b8e-5f97-943b-400b3abc0e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e181f05-c15f-5f8b-9d6d-ca8102d59c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293756} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e2d64a4-38f8-5c58-bd1e-7e22431bd2b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8918c88a-2dbd-5d14-97dd-c946bdc87986" :question/math-academy-id "q-79045" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0\\,⟹\\,x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-x<0,$ which has the solution $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
